# Stage 1: example B1 with a neutron beam, everything P9-P13 owes inactivated, and Decay,
# hadElastic and nCapture LEFT ACTIVE.
#
# **RUN THIS THROUGH ref\b1neutron\run.bat AND NOT THROUGH ref\run\runb1.bat.** It is the one
# macro in this directory that needs a different binary, and the reason is the finding P8d
# wrote up: `EnableNeutronGeneralProcess` has no UI command anywhere in 11.1.1
# (`G4HadronicParametersMessenger` builds three, and it is not one of them), so the flag can
# only be turned off from C++ - one line, in `G4State_PreInit`, after `new QBBC` and before
# `/run/initialize`. `ref/b1neutron/b1neutron.cc` is Geant4's own example B1 with that line and
# nothing else; every other source file in it comes from `$G4SRC/examples/basic/B1`, so this is
# the same B1 every other row of stage1_README.md was measured with.
#
# WHAT THIS MACRO USED TO BE, and why it changed. It used to read
#
#     /process/inactivate NeutronGeneralProc
#
# and the note over it said "THE ONE ROW THAT IS NOT A STAGE-1 CONFIGURATION, and it cannot be
# made one" - because with the flag set, elastic, inelastic and capture are sub-processes of one
# object and `/process/inactivate` takes all three or none. That was true of what could be done
# from a macro and it was not true of what could be done at all. With the flag off, the neutron's
# process manager holds six entries, `neutronInelastic` is one of them, and inactivating it by
# name leaves exactly the processes this port has. docs/RISK.md V53 and V60.
#
# THE SIX ENTRIES, and this macro prints them rather than asserting them (docs/RISK.md V43: ask
# the object). `G4HadProcesses::BuildNeutronElastic` and `BuildNeutronInelasticAndCapture` take
# their `else` branches and register `hadElastic`, `neutronInelastic` and `nCapture` separately,
# and `G4NeutronTrackingCut::ConstructProcess` no longer finds a general process - so it builds
# a real `G4NeutronKiller` with the same 10 us and 0 MeV the general process carries internally.
#
#     Transportation, Decay, hadElastic, neutronInelastic, nCapture, nKiller
#
# `Decay` is left active because a free neutron's 880 s lifetime makes it inert at any energy
# that crosses a phantom, and inactivating an inert process would make the macro differ from
# Geant4's default for no reason.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 1

# The one thing this port does not have for a neutron: the inelastic final state (P9-P11).
# It is a process on the manager now, so this command reaches it; with the general process on
# it answers `illegal process (or type) name`.
/process/inactivate neutronInelastic

# The stage, recorded by what RAN and not by what was intended. This prints Active/InActive for
# every process on the neutron's manager, after the inactivation above - so the six entries in
# the header are a claim the output checks.
/particle/select neutron
/particle/process/dump

/gun/particle neutron
/gun/energy 100 MeV

/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn 500000
